1
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,590
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:10,040
Today I will walk you through our Exposure Lens project and give you a live demo.

3
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:15,317
But before doing that, I would like to start with a short overview of Tenable One

4
00:00:15,317 --> 00:00:18,140
and why identity security has become a must.

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,469
Tenable One is our exposure management platform, and it runs in the cloud,

6
00:00:25,469 --> 00:00:32,280
so its newest capabilities, like AI and cross-domain exposure analysis, are delivered as cloud services.

7
00:00:32,230 --> 00:00:37,410
But many customers cannot move to the cloud because of regulation or data residency.

8
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:43,900
So they run Security Center and Identity Exposure on-premises and miss those capabilities.

9
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:48,380
And they matter: a vulnerability tells only half the story.

10
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:53,301
The real question is who can reach that system and what they can do with it,

11
00:00:53,301 --> 00:00:58,620
because attackers move through identities and privileges, not just vulnerabilities.

12
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:01,817
Under the Tenable Cyber Exchange project, which offers

13
00:01:01,817 --> 00:01:04,008
free assets you can download and use,

14
00:01:04,008 --> 00:01:08,449
we built Exposure Lens to close this gap. We developed it together with our

15
00:01:08,449 --> 00:01:14,620
Professional Services and product teams. It's not an official Tenable product, it's our own project.

16
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:24,580
Let me show you the user interface.

17
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:35,704
When we log in, a dashboard greets us. It summarises vulnerabilities,

18
00:01:35,704 --> 00:01:40,130
risky identities and the hosts they reach, all in one screen.

19
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:46,652
Every widget has a small icon in its top-right corner that explains

20
00:01:46,652 --> 00:01:48,620
how the value is calculated.

21
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,981
Users reaching critical hosts, hosts under Tier 0 accounts,

22
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:02,580
service accounts on critical hosts.

23
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:13,580
I want to talk about integrations.

24
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:18,180
The platform works with several integrations.

25
00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:20,950
Let's start with Tenable Security Center.

26
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:26,683
Tenable Security Center: I click the integration, enter the API URL and the API keys,

27
00:02:26,683 --> 00:02:33,600
and set the vulnerability filters, for example which severities and which repositories to collect.

28
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:39,820
Every finding that matches these filters is brought into the platform.

29
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:47,270
Let's continue with the Identity Exposure integration details.

30
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:54,503
Same approach: I click the integration, enter the API URL and other details,

31
00:02:54,503 --> 00:02:57,370
and define how often the data is synchronised.

32
00:02:58,590 --> 00:03:04,290
Users, groups, computers, group policies and identity findings are all brought in.

33
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:10,980
The last part of the integration is the local LLM.

34
00:03:13,030 --> 00:03:16,790
Finally, I configure a local large language model.

35
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:19,810
In this demo, I use the Gemma 4 model.

36
00:03:19,770 --> 00:03:22,081
The model runs locally, so no data leaves

37
00:03:22,081 --> 00:03:27,010
the environment. That matters a lot for exactly the customers we are talking about.

38
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:31,310
Once these settings are in place, the data pools take shape on the platform.

39
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:40,240
I want to show you the AI assistant, Agent Tenable.

40
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:53,686
I can type here in Turkish or English. For example, let me ask:

41
00:03:53,686 --> 00:03:55,510
investigate this user.

42
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:08,666
It's using our agentic harness: tools, skills and sub-agent

43
00:04:08,666 --> 00:04:11,660
definitions for collecting these data sets.

44
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:24,500
It shows me Ali Okan, the e-mail address, the organisational unit.

45
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:32,915
If we have findings about password security, for example if this user is using a weak password,

46
00:04:32,915 --> 00:04:36,260
I can see all of these details here, as you can see.

47
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:46,600
Each investigation also creates an asset card. If I want, I can just click to open it

48
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:49,780
and see all the details of the user asset here.

49
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:57,780
It could be a computer asset as well.

50
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:01,900
I can see connected assets and vulnerabilities.

51
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:07,950
By the way, I can group them by host or by CVE.

52
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:16,734
I can see the risk details, why this user is risky. All the details are

53
00:05:16,734 --> 00:05:18,740
shown in one asset card.

54
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:29,505
Correlation evidence gives me all the insights about the correlation:

55
00:05:29,505 --> 00:05:34,310
why this user is related to these specific findings, and I can also see the groups of the

56
00:05:34,310 --> 00:05:35,100
user here.

57
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:46,300
I can perform this analysis for several assets.

58
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:47,610
I can filter.

59
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:51,860
We also added some additional capabilities, like local users.

60
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:58,650
You can just click the Local Users link and see all local accounts in your environment.

61
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:05,960
We also built a software inventory and a browser extensions data set.

62
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:15,200
You can list and filter those findings.

63
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,560
These are findings coming from SC.

64
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:34,438
To summarise, Exposure Lens brings identity-aware, AI-assisted vulnerability management

65
00:06:34,438 --> 00:06:40,016
to on-premises environments. It combines Security Center and Identity Exposure data,

66
00:06:40,016 --> 00:06:44,450
and it uses a local LLM, so the data stays in your environment.

67
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:45,650
Thank you for listening.

68
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:47,160
I'm happy to take your questions.
